<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- App Store Connect distribution.

         `destination: export` writes an .ipa so it can be inspected; switch to
         `upload` to send straight to TestFlight. Kept on `export` because an
         accidental upload is worse than an extra edit.

         The full ship, as actually run for 0.3 (3) on 2026-08-31:

           xcodebuild -project ios/Slab3.xcodeproj -scheme Slab3 \
             -destination 'generic/platform=iOS' \
             -archivePath <path>.xcarchive -allowProvisioningUpdates archive
           xcodebuild -exportArchive -archivePath <path>.xcarchive \
             -exportOptionsPlist ios/ExportOptions.plist -exportPath <dir> \
             -authenticationKeyPath ~/.appstoreconnect/private_keys/AuthKey_F2F97BLXVN.p8 \
             -authenticationKeyID F2F97BLXVN -authenticationKeyIssuerID <issuer-uuid>

         SIGNING IS MANUAL ON PURPOSE (was: automatic). Automatic signing needs
         Xcode's CLOUD signing to mint a certificate and regenerate the profile,
         and the App Store Connect API key is refused for it — cloud signing wants
         an Admin/App Manager role key, and this one is not. It can still read and
         CREATE certificates and profiles through the API, which is how the pair
         below was made. Symptoms when this regresses to automatic:
             "Cloud signing permission error"
             "No signing certificate iOS Distribution found"
             "Provisioning profile … doesn't include signing certificate …"
         Upgrading the key to App Manager would let automatic signing work again.

         The certificate below is the one whose PRIVATE KEY lives on the ship
         machine's login keychain. The account's older iOS Distribution cert
         (expires 2027-05-05) was created elsewhere, so its key is not here and it
         cannot sign from this machine — a certificate without its key is inert.
         If you ship from a different Mac, that Mac needs its own key: either
         export this identity as a .p12, or create another cert and a profile
         naming it. -->
    <key>method</key>
    <string>app-store-connect</string>
    <key>destination</key>
    <string>export</string>
    <key>teamID</key>
    <string>7BMY83MWA8</string>
    <key>signingStyle</key>
    <string>manual</string>
    <key>signingCertificate</key>
    <string>iPhone Distribution: varun aravind (7BMY83MWA8)</string>
    <!-- Created via the ASC API 2026-08-31 and installed to
         ~/Library/MobileDevice/Provisioning Profiles/. Only the shipping target
         is listed; SlabsDev is a development build and never goes through here. -->
    <key>provisioningProfiles</key>
    <dict>
        <key>com.varunaravind.slabs</key>
        <string>Slabs AppStore 20260831</string>
    </dict>
    <!-- dSYMs, so TestFlight crash reports symbolicate. -->
    <key>uploadSymbols</key>
    <true/>
    <key>manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
